## Rule details

### Rule 1 — One source of truth

Bad: `memory/05-theming.md` and `memory/14-dark-theme.md` both list dark
tokens; the two tables disagree.

Good: `memory/14-dark-theme.md` is canonical. `memory/05-theming.md` has one
line: *"For dark mode tokens see `memory/14-dark-theme.md`."*

Applies to: token tables, section lists, hard rules, color palettes, motion
durations, asset lists.

### Rule 2 — H1 = filename prefix

`memory/07-reduced-motion.md` opens:

```
# 07 · Reduced motion — USE THIS WHEN: shipping animation, transitions,
# or scroll-driven effects and need to gate the OS-level preference.
```

If you find the H1 number drifting from the filename, fix one or the other —
never ship a mismatch.

### Rule 3 — Monotonic filenames

If `memory/04-cinematic-hero.md` and `memory/04-locale-handoff.md` collide,
renumber the later addition (locale-handoff → `10-locale-handoff.md`) rather
than inserting between. Inserting forces every consumer to re-grep.

### Rule 4 — Grep-verifiable claims

Every concrete assertion in `00-readme-first.md` becomes a line in
`tests/verify.sh`:

```bash
# README claim: "sections use data-section attribute"
test_data_section_attr() {
  rg -q 'data-section=' skeleton/index.html || {
    echo "FAIL: skeleton missing data-section attribute"; exit 1
  }
}

test_data_section_attr
```

Add one test per claim. The script exits non-zero on any failure. Run it
before every commit.

### Rule 5 — Manifest only references existing files

```text
# BAD
agents_manager/assets/SKILL.md          # not in working tree
agents_manager/assets/notes/branch-decisions.md   # not in working tree

# GOOD
skeleton/index.html
skeleton/index.css
assets/fonts/inter.woff2
```

If a cross-tree reference is genuinely needed (e.g., to a proposed specialist),
mark it explicitly:

```text
agents_manager/assets/SKILL.md  (proposed — see PROPOSAL F2)
```

…and ensure the proposal is tracked in `decision-log.md`.

### Rule 6 — Trigger lines on memory files

The trigger line is what an agent greps for when deciding whether to load the
file. Format:

```
# NN · <topic> — USE THIS WHEN: <imperative one-liner>
```

Examples:

- `# 04 · Cinematic hero — USE THIS WHEN: designing the hero canvas and need
  cutout/aura coordination`
- `# 07 · Reduced motion — USE THIS WHEN: shipping animation or scroll-driven
  effects`
- `# 11 · Canvas a11y — USE THIS WHEN: shipping a `<canvas>` that conveys
  meaning and needs a screen-reader fallback`

No file is allowed to ship without a trigger line.

### Rule 7 — Skeleton obeys its own memory

If `memory/04-cinematic-hero.md` says *"never use the same image with
`filter: blur()` applied"*, then `skeleton/index.html` does not contain such a
pair. The skeleton is the test oracle for the memory's hard rules.

When a skeleton needs to deviate, the memory is updated first, with a `P<n>`
fix reference in the decision log.

### Rule 8 — Acceptance checklist

Before publishing or merging a template, every item must be ticked:

- [ ] `INDEX.md` exists; every key convention is listed in one place
- [ ] All greppable claims in `00-readme-first.md` pass `tests/verify.sh`
- [ ] Every memory file's H1 number matches its filename prefix
- [ ] Filenames are monotonic (no gaps, no collisions)
- [ ] Every line of `assets/MANIFEST.txt` resolves in the working tree
- [ ] Skeleton obeys every hard rule in `memory/`
- [ ] `decision-log.md` has at least one entry per phase (proposed / accepted / deprecated)
- [ ] At least one worked example + its abstract recipe (see "Teach the recipe")
- [ ] Reduced-motion path tested manually: toggle OS setting, reload, observe
- [ ] a11y floor clean (axe-core or equivalent on light + dark variants)
- [ ] No token table duplicated across files (Rule 1)
- [ ] If multi-locale: `locales/` exists with at least one non-default script

